Meningitis & Encephalitis
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
High fever/severe headache, Profound nuchal rigidity, Altered mental status, Elevated WBCs in cerebrospinal fluid

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-028
Reason for Visit: Abrupt onset of unrelenting, excruciating headache, high fever, and extreme neck stiffness requiring urgent neurological assessment for acute central nervous system infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a young adult experiencing a rapidly progressive, severe generalized headache with extreme photophobia and an inability to bend the neck. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate for acute bacterial meningitis or encephalitis, rule out impending septicaemia, and facilitate immediate, life-saving emergency medical transfer prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is in absolute agony, the light is blinding me, and I can't move my neck at all."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced extremely abruptly 8 hours ago with a high fever frequently accompanied by severe, shaking chills, and an unrelenting generalized headache. Over the last 4 hours, the patient has developed profound, progressive systemic lethargy and extreme malaise. The patient reports excruciating photophobia resulting in strict avoidance of bright light. The roommate notes the patient has been mildly confused and unusually repetitive over the last hour. There is a complete inability to touch the chin to the chest due to pain. No recent travel or known sick contacts.
Functional Status: Bedbound, lying perfectly still in a darkened room, moaning softly.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapid neurological decompensation and mortality; roommate is present and appropriately alarmed.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 | HR: 125 | Temp: 39.8 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Toxic, ashen appearance. Patient is actively shielding eyes with a pillow and refusing to move their head.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals mild altered mental status (GCS 14 - confused). Profound nuchal rigidity is present, manifesting as an extreme physical inability to passively flex the neck. Positive Kernig's sign (severe pain in the hamstrings and resistance on passive knee extension with the hip flexed) and positive Brudzinski's sign (involuntary hip and knee flexion upon passive neck flexion). Skin exam reveals scattered, early non-blanching petechial lesions on the lower extremities, highly indicative of evolving meningococcal septicaemia. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: High fever/severe headache, Profound nuchal rigidity, Altered mental status, and Elevated WBCs in cerebrospinal fluid (pending urgent lumbar puncture in hospital).
Environmental Observations: Curtains tightly drawn due to severe photophobia.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Unvaccinated for Meningococcal ACWY/B.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Bacterial Meningitis (suspected Meningococcal with early septicaemia) - Status: Exacerbated (Critical Emergency)
Differentials:

Subarachnoid Haemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent infectious prodrome (high fever, chills) and the presence of a petechial rash, which point definitively toward an infectious etiology, despite the severe sudden headache.

Viral Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of altered mental status, extreme toxicity, and the non-blanching petechial rash, which are red flags for a severe bacterial process.

Migraine with Aura - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of high fever, frank nuchal rigidity, Kernig's/Brudzinski's signs, and an evolving petechial rash.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Time-critical life-threatening emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic, fulminant triad of acute bacterial meningitis: fever, nuchal rigidity, and altered mental status. Crucially, the discovery of a non-blanching petechial rash on physical examination indicates impending, catastrophic meningococcal septicaemia. This is an absolute, time-critical medical emergency with a high mortality rate measured in hours. An immediate, urgent on-site clinical evaluation was mandatory to recognize the classic meningeal signs, identify the septic rash, and coordinate a priority ambulance transfer for life-saving intravenous antibiotics (Ceftriaxone) before vascular collapse or severe cerebral edema occurs. This presentation strictly satisfies and exceeds the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: IMMEDIATE call to 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport. Administer stat intramuscular or intravenous Ceftriaxone 2g immediately (if available in the physician's bag) prior to transfer; do not wait for the ambulance if the drug is on hand. Patient requires urgent hospital admission for a lumbar puncture, blood cultures, and ICU-level monitoring.
Positive Behavioral Change: Not applicable for the patient due to altered mental status. Directed roommate to gather the patient's ID and clear a path for paramedics.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory notification to the State Public Health Unit regarding a suspected meningococcal case for immediate contact tracing and prophylaxis of close contacts (including the roommate).
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to receiving Emergency Department physician and paramedics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. Roommate instructed to place the patient in the recovery position immediately if they begin to vomit or experience a generalized tonic-clonic seizure to prevent fatal airway aspiration.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is highly suspected acute meningococcal meningitis/septicaemia; the patient is critically unstable.
Clinical Course: This is a life-threatening infection of the brain lining and bloodstream that requires immediate intensive care support and intravenous antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

(To Roommate): Do not try to give him any water or painkillers by mouth. His brain is swelling and his mental state is fluctuating; he will choke. Keep the room pitch black and quiet until the paramedics walk through the door.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: (To Roommate): A highly aggressive bacteria has infected the fluid surrounding his brain and spinal cord, and it is starting to spread into his bloodstream, which is causing the purple spots on his legs. He needs massive doses of specialized intravenous antibiotics immediately at the hospital to stop the swelling in his brain and prevent his organs from failing.
Actionable Steps: You (the roommate) will likely need preventative antibiotics tomorrow as a close contact. Wait for the paramedics. Do not move the patient unnecessarily.

